WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.100
One task list, a blocked calendar, and messages on your terms.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.510
This is the video for Module 2 of Productivity and Time Management at Work.

3
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:19.830
Many of us keep work in our heads, on scraps of paper, and in starred WhatsApp chats. The result is a constant feeling of something forgotten.

4
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:27.900
In this module you give every task a home, give important work a time, and decide when you deal with messages.

5
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:40.720
David Allen, author of Getting Things Done, says the mind is for having ideas, not holding them. So first, capture everything into one inbox, such as a pinned Google Keep note.

6
00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:46.720
Then clarify each item. If it is not actionable, delete it or file it.

7
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.130
If the next action takes less than two minutes, do it now.

8
00:00:52.380 --> 00:01:02.710
Otherwise, put it in the right list in Google Tasks. Next actions, Waiting for, or Projects. Only things tied to a day or time go into Google Calendar.

9
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:13.560
Staff welfare is not an action, so it sits on a list for weeks. Draft three questions for the staff welfare survey is a next action. Start with a verb.

10
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.520
On Monday morning Grace feels overwhelmed and cannot say what is on her plate.

11
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:28.430
In 15 minutes she writes 31 items into a Keep note, from her notebook, starred WhatsApp messages and two sticky notes.

12
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:38.900
She deletes or files 4 and does 6 small ones on the spot. The rest become 13 next actions, 5 projects, and 3 follow-ups.

13
00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:43.090
In about 45 minutes, she knows exactly what to do first.

14
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:52.570
A task list says what. A calendar says when. A task with a time is far more likely to happen. Put fixed meetings in first.

15
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.880
Then block 60 to 120 minutes for your most important task, in your best-energy hours.

16
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:04.780
Name it with the action, like Write board report section 2.

17
00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:15.200
Batching means doing similar tasks together, like all calls, or all supplier payments, in one block. Each switch costs you time getting back in.

18
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:22.270
And leave a buffer. When the plan breaks, and it will, take two minutes to re-block the rest of the day.

19
00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:34.900
The Pomodoro Technique was named after a tomato-shaped kitchen timer. Work on one task for 25 minutes without switching. If a thought interrupts you, write it in your inbox.

20
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.640
Then take a short break of about 5 minutes.

21
00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:43.840
After four sessions, take a longer break of 15 to 30 minutes.

22
00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:55.690
For example, Chioma, a sales manager in Abuja, runs her monthly sales review as four Pomodoros from 9 to 11, and finishes by 11 instead of late at night.

23
00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:04.920
For many of us, WhatsApp is where work happens. The goal is not to ignore people. It is to decide when you deal with messages.

24
00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:13.560
Open them in a batch, and give each one a decision. Delete it, do it if it takes under two minutes, delegate it, or defer it to a task.

25
00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:21.850
Bukola's baseline showed 28 WhatsApp interruptions a day, and only 30 minutes of focused menu planning.

26
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:31.380
She moves customer chats to WhatsApp Business and creates 5 quick replies, such as her price list, delivery areas and how to pay.

27
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:43.750
She mutes 6 non-urgent groups and checks them once a day. She answers messages at 8, 12:30 and 4, and her profile tells customers to call for emergencies.

28
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:55.000
After two weeks her interruptions fall to about 6 a day, and she gets close to 2 focused hours each morning. Customers still get answers within a few hours.

29
00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:09.610
Batching also gives you time to check messages calmly. Be suspicious of urgent payment requests, new account numbers from suppliers, or messages claiming to be from your bank. Call a number you already know.

30
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:18.280
And never share OTPs or PINs on WhatsApp. Do not keep passwords or your BVN in Keep notes either.

31
00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:35.790
To remember. Capture into one inbox and clarify every item. Write next actions that start with a verb. Block your most important task first. Focus in 25-minute sprints. And batch your messages, giving each one of the four Ds.

32
00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:45.510
Now your turn. Build your task system, time-block three working days, and set up message control. Then tally your interruptions again.

33
00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.800
Continue with the lessons below.
